      <div purpose="landing-page-section">
        <div purpose="section-heading">
          <h2>What is Windows MDM?</h2>
          <p>Windows mobile device management (MDM) is the software and protocols IT teams use to enroll, configure, secure, and report on Windows 10 and Windows 11 devices at scale. A Windows MDM platform applies configuration through <a href="/guides/creating-windows-csps">Windows Configuration Service Providers (CSPs)</a>, enforces disk encryption with BitLocker, distributes software and OS updates, and reports back on device posture. Fleet is an <a href="/windows">AI-native, open source Windows MDM</a> that does this across Windows, macOS, Linux, iOS, iPadOS, Android, and ChromeOS from a single platform. AI drafts each change, and your team approves it before it touches a device.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div purpose="landing-page-section">
        <div purpose="section-heading">
          <h3>Managing Windows shouldn’t mean choosing a silo</h3>
          <p>Most Windows management tools assume your fleet is Microsoft-only. They make it harder to enforce posture, prove compliance, and work across the rest of the operating systems your business actually runs.</p>
        </div>
        <div purpose="four-column-feature-row">
          <div purpose="feature-item">
            <h5>Windows-only silos</h5>
            <p>Single-platform tools force IT to run separate consoles for Macs, Linux, and mobile, with separate inventories.</p>
          </div>
          <div purpose="feature-item">
            <h5>Slow, risky changes</h5>
            <p>Changes happen by hand in a console, with no review and no history. One bad policy rolls out silently, and rolling back is a ticket, not an undo.</p>
          </div>
          <div purpose="feature-item">
            <h5>Cached, stale visibility</h5>
            <p>Periodic check-ins miss the real-time posture security teams and auditors need to see across every device.</p>
          </div>
          <div purpose="feature-item">
            <h5>Paying to set up a laptop</h5>
            <p>Autopilot requires Microsoft Entra ID P1 for every user, even if your company already chose Okta or Google for identity.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div purpose="landing-page-section">
        <div purpose="section-heading">
          <h2>Zero touch, $0</h2>
          <p>Ship new Windows PCs straight to employees. No Autopilot, no Entra ID, and no Intune. Fleet supports Autopilot and Entra if you want them, but zero touch with Fleet needs no Microsoft license.</p>
        </div>
        <div purpose="feature-row">
          <div purpose="feature-item">
            <h5>Forward the order email</h5>
            <p>Fleet gives you an order email. Forward it to Dell, Lenovo, or HP with your next hardware order.</p>
          </div>
          <div purpose="feature-item">
            <h5>They install Fleet before the PC ships</h5>
            <p>Your hardware provider installs Fleet at the factory, so every new PC arrives already managed by Fleet. Foldables, Copilot+ PCs, and Arm laptops included.</p>
          </div>
          <div purpose="feature-item">
            <h5>Your employee opens the lid</h5>
            <p>The PC enrolls the first time it’s online. Fleet installs software, applies settings, and turns on BitLocker. Nobody in IT touches it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div purpose="section-heading">
          <p>Proven in production on thousands of Windows PCs. Moving off Intune? For qualifying teams, Fleet covers your hardware provider’s one-time setup fee for new PCs. <a href="/articles/autopilot-without-autopilot">See how it works</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div purpose="landing-page-section">
        <div purpose="feature-with-image">
          <div purpose="feature-image">
            <img alt="A screenshot of Fleet managing Windows OS settings across a fleet of devices." src="/images/articles/fleet-windows-mdm-launch-os-settings-800x640@2x.png">
          </div>
          <div purpose="feature-text">
            <h3>Manage Windows with the visibility and control modern IT teams need</h3>
            <p>Fleet is <a href="/lp/open-source">open source</a>, AI-native Windows device management built on the <a href="/guides/windows-mdm-setup">Windows MDM protocol</a> and osquery. Enroll, configure, encrypt, and patch every Windows device without giving up transparency or cross-platform support.</p>
            <div purpose="checklist">
              <p>Zero touch, $0. Your <a href="/guides/windows-mdm-setup#zero-touch-enrollment-with-an-oem-service">hardware provider installs Fleet</a> before each PC ships. No Autopilot, Entra ID P1, or Intune required.</p>
              <p>Enforce BitLocker, configuration profiles, and OS settings with prompts like “Disable camera in Firefox.”</p>
              <p>Patch Windows, install Fleet-maintained apps, and run remediation scripts on demand.</p>
              <p>Continuously evaluate CIS Benchmarks for Windows and surface vulnerabilities in real time.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div purpose="landing-page-section">
        <div purpose="section-quote">
          <div purpose="quote-image">
            <img alt="Benchling logo" src="/images/social-proof-logo-benchling-143x32@2x.png">
          </div>
          <p purpose="quote-text">"Seeing industry-standard tools like osquery and git combined with MDM and CI/CD to manage endpoints was impressive. With support for Linux, macOS, Windows, iOS, iPadOS, and Chrome OS, managing everything from a single codebase is a game-changer."</p>
          <div purpose="quote-author-info">
            <div purpose="quote-author-image">
              <img alt="Justin LaBo" src="/images/testimonial-author-justin-labo-48x48@2x.png">
            </div>
            <div purpose="name-and-title">
              <p purpose="author-name">Justin LaBo</p>
              <p purpose="author-title">Head of Core BT Engineering</p>
              <p purpose="author-title">Benchling</p>
            </div>
